A f3-007 WRONG {'text': "To determine how much energy a device or component stores in **millijoules (mJ)**, we need to know:\n\n1. **The capacitance (C)** in farads (F), if it's a capacitor.\n2. **The voltage (V)** across the capacitor.\n3. The **inductance (L)** and **current (I)**, if it's an inductor.\n4. The **mass (m)** and **specific energy (E)**, if it's a battery or other energy storage device.\n\n### Formula for Energy Stored in a Capacitor:\n$$\nE = \\frac{1}{2} C V^2\n$$\n\n### Formula for Energy Stored in an Inductor:\n$$\nE = \\frac{1}{2} L I^2\n$$\n\n### Formula for Energy Stored in a Battery (approximate):\n$$\nE = V \\times I \\times t\n$$\nor\n$$\nE = \\text{Capacity (Ah)} \\times \\text{Voltage (V)} \\times 3600 \\text{ (to convert to joules)}\n$$\n\n### Converting to Millijoules:\n$$\n1 \\text{ joule} = 1000 \\text{ millijoules}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Example:\nIf you have a **capacitor** with **C = 100 μF (0.0001 F)** and **V = 10 V**, then:\n\n$$\nE = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.0001 \\times 10^2 = 0.0005 \\text{ J} = 0.5 \\text{ mJ}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\nPlease provide the **type of energy storage device** (capacitor, inductor, battery, etc.), along with its **parameters** (voltage, current, capacitance, inductance, etc.), and I will calculate the energy in **millijoules** for you."}
