A f3-073 WRONG {'text': "To determine the **glide angle** in degrees, we need more information, such as:\n\n- The **horizontal distance traveled** (runway length or glide path distance)\n- The **vertical drop** (altitude lost during the glide)\n- The **aircraft's speed or time of descent**\n\nHowever, if we assume a **standard glide ratio** (common for gliders or aircraft in a glide without power), we can calculate the glide angle using basic trigonometry.\n\n---\n\n### 🔢 Formula for Glide Angle:\n\n$$\n\\text{Glide Angle} = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{\\text{Vertical Drop}}{\\text{Horizontal Distance}}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives the angle in **radians**, so to convert to **degrees**, use:\n\n$$\n\\text{Glide Angle (in degrees)} = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{\\text{Vertical Drop}}{\\text{Horizontal Distance}}\\right) \\times \\frac{180}{\\pi}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### 🧮 Example:\n\nSuppose an aircraft glides **1000 meters horizontally** while **losing 200 meters in altitude**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Glide Angle} = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{200}{1000}\\right) = \\arctan(0.2) \\approx 11.31^\\circ\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer (if you provide more details):\n\nIf you can share the **horizontal distance** and **vertical drop**, I can calculate the exact glide angle for you. Otherwise, the **standard glide angle for a glider** is typically around **10 to 12 degrees**, depending on the glide ratio.\n\nWould you like to provide more specific data?"}
