A f3-076 WRONG {'text': "To calculate the **orbital speed** of an object (like a satellite or planet), we need more specific information, such as:\n\n- The **mass of the central body** (e.g., Earth, Sun, etc.)\n- The **distance from the center of the central body** (i.e., the orbital radius)\n- The **gravitational constant** $ G $\n- Possibly the **mass of the orbiting object** (though it often cancels out in the formula)\n\nThe formula for **orbital speed** is:\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ v $ is the orbital speed (in km/s),\n- $ G $ is the gravitational constant ($ 6.67430 \\times 10^{-11} \\, \\text{m}^3 \\text{kg}^{-1} \\text{s}^{-2} $),\n- $ M $ is the mass of the central body (in kg),\n- $ r $ is the orbital radius (in meters).\n\n---\n\n### Example: Orbital Speed of the Moon around Earth\n\n- $ M_{\\text{Earth}} = 5.972 \\times 10^{24} \\, \\text{kg} $\n- $ r = 384,400 \\, \\text{km} = 3.844 \\times 10^8 \\, \\text{m} $\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{(6.67430 \\times 10^{-11}) \\times (5.972 \\times 10^{24})}{3.844 \\times 10^8}}\n$$\n\n$$\nv \\approx \\sqrt{1.023 \\times 10^{16}} \\approx 1.01 \\, \\text{km/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Example: Orbital Speed of the International Space Station (ISS) around Earth\n\n- $ r = 400 \\, \\text{km} = 4.00 \\times 10^5 \\, \\text{m} $\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{(6.67430 \\times 10^{-11}) \\times (5.972 \\times 10^{24})}{4.00 \\times 10^5}} \\approx 7.8 \\, \\text{km/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Example: Orbital Speed of Earth around the Sun\n\n- $ M_{\\text{Sun}} = 1.989 \\times 10^{30} \\, \\text{kg} $\n- $ r = 149.6 \\times 10^6 \\, \\text{km} = 1.496 \\times 10^{11} \\, \\text{m} $\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{(6.67430 \\times 10^{-11}) \\times (1.989 \\times 10^{30})}{1.496 \\times 10^{11}}} \\approx 29.78 \\, \\text{km/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nIf you provide the specific **mass** of the central body and the **orbital radius**, I can calculate the **orbital speed** in km/s for you.\n\nLet me know the details, and I'll compute it for you!"}
