A f3-090 WRONG {'text': 'The **Mach number (M)** is a dimensionless quantity that represents the ratio of the **local flow velocity** to the **speed of sound** in the surrounding medium. It is used in aerodynamics and fluid mechanics to describe how fast a fluid is moving relative to the speed of sound in that fluid.\n\n### Definition:\n$$\nM = \\frac{v}{a}\n$$\nWhere:\n- $ v $ is the velocity of the fluid (or object) in m/s,\n- $ a $ is the speed of sound in the medium (in m/s).\n\n---\n\n### Speed of Sound in a Perfect Gas:\nFor a perfect gas, the speed of sound $ a $ is given by:\n$$\na = \\sqrt{\\gamma R T}\n$$\nWhere:\n- $ \\gamma $ is the specific heat ratio (also called the adiabatic index),\n- $ R $ is the specific gas constant (in J/(kg·K)),\n- $ T $ is the absolute temperature (in K).\n\n---\n\n### Given:\n- $ \\gamma = 1.4 $\n- $ R = 287 \\, \\text{J/(kg·K)} $\n\n---\n\n### Example Calculation (if you provide temperature or velocity):\n\nLet’s say you have a temperature $ T = 300 \\, \\text{K} $, and you want to find the speed of sound:\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{1.4 \\cdot 287 \\cdot 300} = \\sqrt{119, 940} \\approx 346.3 \\, \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\nIf the velocity of the fluid is, say, $ v = 346.3 \\, \\text{m/s} $, then the Mach number is:\n\n$$\nM = \\frac{346.3}{346.3} = 1\n$$\n\nSo the Mach number is **1**, which means the flow is **sonic**.\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n- **Mach number (M)** = $ \\frac{v}{a} $\n- Speed of sound $ a = \\sqrt{\\gamma R T} $\n- With $ \\gamma = 1.4 $ and $ R = 287 \\, \\text{J/(kg·K)} $, you can compute $ a $ for any temperature $ T $, and then calculate the Mach number for a given velocity $ v $.\n\nLet me know if you want to calculate the Mach number for a specific velocity or temperature.'}
