B f3-073 WRONG {'text': "To find the **glide angle** of a glider, we can use the **lift-to-drag ratio (L/D)**, which relates the horizontal distance the glider can travel to the vertical distance it descends.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Understand the relationship between glide angle and L/D**\n\nThe **glide angle (θ)** is the angle between the glider's flight path and the horizontal. It is related to the **lift-to-drag ratio (L/D)** by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\text{L/D}}\n$$\n\nThis is because the lift-to-drag ratio (L/D) is the ratio of the horizontal distance (lift) to the vertical distance (drag), so the tangent of the glide angle is the inverse of that ratio.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Plug in the given value**\n\nGiven:\n- Lift-to-drag ratio (L/D) = 40\n\n$$\n\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{40} = 0.025\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Find the glide angle in degrees**\n\nNow, take the arctangent (inverse tangent) of 0.025:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(0.025)\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\theta \\approx 1.433^\\circ\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1.43^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThis means the glider will descend **1.43 degrees** from the horizontal for every unit of horizontal distance traveled."}
