B f3-078 WRONG {'text': "To calculate the **escape speed** from a celestial body (like a planet or star), we use the formula:\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 G M}{r}}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $ v_e $ is the escape speed (in km/s)\n- $ G $ is the gravitational constant: $ 6.67430 \\times 10^{-11} \\, \\text{m}^3 \\text{kg}^{-1} \\text{s}^{-2} $\n- $ M $ is the mass of the celestial body (in kg)\n- $ r $ is the radius of the celestial body (in meters)\n\nHowever, **you haven't provided the mass of the celestial body**. If you're referring to the **Earth**, for example, we can proceed with the known values:\n\n---\n\n### **Assuming it's Earth:**\n\n- Radius $ r = 8000 \\, \\text{km} = 8,000,000 \\, \\text{m} $\n- Earth's mass $ M = 5.972 \\times 10^{24} \\, \\text{kg} $\n\nNow plug into the escape speed formula:\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 6.6743 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 5.972 \\times 10^{24}}{8,000,000}}\n$$\n\nLet's compute this step by step:\n\n1. Multiply $ 2 \\times 6.6743 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 5.972 \\times 10^{24} $:\n\n$$\n= 2 \\times 6.6743 \\times 5.972 \\times 10^{-11 + 24} = 2 \\times 6.6743 \\times 5.972 \\times 10^{13}\n$$\n\n$$\n= 2 \\times 39.96 \\times 10^{13} = 79.92 \\times 10^{13} = 7.992 \\times 10^{14}\n$$\n\n2. Divide by $ 8,000,000 = 8 \\times 10^6 $:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7.992 \\times 10^{14}}{8 \\times 10^6} = \\frac{7.992}{8} \\times 10^{14 - 6} = 0.999 \\times 10^8 = 9.99 \\times 10^7\n$$\n\n3. Take the square root:\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{9.99 \\times 10^7} \\approx 9.995 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{m/s} = 9.995 \\, \\text{km/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{10.0 \\, \\text{km/s}}\n$$\n\nThis is the **escape speed** from a surface at a radius of 8000 km, assuming Earth's mass.\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Note:\nIf the object is **not Earth**, the escape speed will be **different**. You would need to provide the mass or the gravitational acceleration at that radius to compute the correct value."}
